WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
SPEAKER: Last time, you saw
how Cloud operations can

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.370
help you quickly isolate or
eliminate infrastructure issues

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.960
from a limited set of data.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.790
You also saw how
to quickly filter

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.670
alerts to determine whether
your service is being impacted

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.205
by an infrastructure issue.

00:00:13.205 --> 00:00:15.330
But how can you identify
problems with your service

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.122
itself?

00:00:16.122 --> 00:00:17.580
And when there's
a problem, how can

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.592
you quickly triage
and mitigate it?

00:00:19.592 --> 00:00:22.050
Today, I'm going to show you
how to manage services running

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.180
on GKE with Cloud Operations.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.452
Welcome to "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:00:27.452 --> 00:00:30.164
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.205
Does this look familiar?

00:00:32.205 --> 00:00:34.580
It's really hard to determine
if your service is actually

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.100
healthy from infrastructure
signals like these.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.050
In chapter 6 of the
SRE book, we say

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.080
that your monitoring system
should address two questions--

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.420
what's broken and why.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.460
Signals from your
infrastructure may tell you

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.200
why something is
happening, but they

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.750
don't tell you anything about
the reliability of your service

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.060
itself as experienced by users.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.060
To understand that, you need
good Service Level Indicators

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.480
or SLIs that measure service
reliability and Service Level

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.510
Objectives or SLOs that set
specific reliability targets.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.060
We looked at how to configure
an SLO for a service running

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.560
on GKE by using load balancer
metrics back in episode two.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.980
We'll link to it if you didn't
get a chance to watch it.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.025
So what happens when
there's a problem?

00:01:15.025 --> 00:01:16.900
Let's have a look at
how Cloud operations can

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.010
help you figure out what to do.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.625
Let's start on the
services overview page.

00:01:22.625 --> 00:01:24.750
We can see that we have a
service with an SLO alert

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.610
firing.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.070
In this case, our service
is running on GKE.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.660
Let's have a look
at the GKE Dashboard

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.160
to see what's going on.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.410
Here, we can see that
we have an unhealthy SLO

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.650
for this Kubernetes service.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.530
Let's have a closer look.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.160
Here's our SLO and
the alert letting

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.580
us know we're burning
error budget right now.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.190
We can also confirm that our
SLI is severely degraded.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.270
What could be happening?

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.520
In the Metrics tab, we
can see that the pod

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.080
name was recently changed.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.960
And this seems to correspond
to when the alert fired,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.520
so something has changed.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.740
Let's try to figure out what.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.590
Let's go to the GKE Workload
screen and look at the workload

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.690
that we're running.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.612
When we look at the revision
history for workload,

00:02:10.612 --> 00:02:13.070
we can see that a new revision
was rolled out a few minutes

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.750
before the alert fired.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.370
This may be our culprit.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.960
Let's roll this
release back and see

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.940
if that restores our service
back to a healthy state.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.710
Now that we rolled
back that release,

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.660
let's check the state
of our workload.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.570
Under Revision History, we can
confirm that we've rolled back

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.840
to a known good version.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.200
Let's go to Services and
confirm that the error budget

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.423
burn alert is no longer firing.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.090
When we check the
status of our service,

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.420
we can see that the SLO is
no longer firing an alert.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.270
And our SLI has also recovered.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.340
So, we were able to
identify a problem

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.590
with our service running
on GKE, determined

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.240
that it was caused
by bad rollout,

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.040
and rolled back to
a known good state

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.350
to recover the health
of our service.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.740
Thanks for joining me today.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.190
We saw how Cloud Operations
can help you quickly triage

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:03.650
and mitigate application issues
for services running on GKE.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.860
I hope this helps you keep
your applications reliable

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.420
and keep your users happy.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.643
Thanks so much for watching.

00:03:08.643 --> 00:03:10.810
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.270
on more "Engineering for
Reliability with a Google

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:13.840
Cloud."

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:14.440
See you soon.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

